 Sick Industrial Companies (Special Provisions) Act, 1985 (1 of 1986) where such sick industrial company is being managed by its workers co-operative :

Provided that such transfer is made during the period commencing from the previous year in which the said company has become a sick industrial company under sub-section (1) of section 17 65 of that Act and ending with the previous year during which the entire net worth of such company becomes equal to or exceeds the accumulated losses. Explanation. For the purposes of this clause, net worth shall have the meaning assigned to it in clause (ga) of sub-section (1) of section 3 65 of the Sick Industrial Companies (Special Provisions) Act, 1985 (1 of 1986).]

The following clauses (xiii) to (xv) shall be inserted after clause (xii) of section 47 by the Finance (No. 2) Act, 1998, w.e.f. 1-4-1999 :

(xiii) where a firm is succeeded by a company in the business carried on by it as a result of which the firm sells or otherwise transfers any capital asset or intangible asset to the company :

Provided that

(a) all the assets and liabilities of the firm relating to the business immediately before the succession become the assets and liabilities of the company;

(b) all the partners of the firm immediately before the succession become the shareholders of the company in the same proportion in which their capital accounts stood in the books of the firm on the date of succession;

(c) the partners of the firm do not receive any consideration or benefit, directly or indirectly, in any form or manner, other than by way of allotment of shares in the company; and

(d) the aggregate of the shareholding in the company of the partners of the firm is not less than fifty per cent of the total voting power in the company and their shareholding continues to be as such for a period of five years from the date of the succession;

(xiv) where a sole proprietary concern is succeeded by a company in the business carried on by it as a result of which the sole proprietary concern sells or otherwise transfers any capital asset or intangible asset to the company :

Provided that

(a) all the assets and liabilities of the sole proprietary concern relating to the business immediately before the succession become the assets and liabilities of the company;

(b) the shareholding of the sole proprietor in the company is not less than fifty per cent of the total voting power in the company and his shareholding continues to remain as such for a period of five years from the date of the succession; and

(c) the sole proprietor does not receive any consideration or benefit, directly or indirectly, in any form or manner, other than by way of allotment of shares in the company;

(xv) any transfer in a scheme for lending of any securities under an agreement or arrangement, which the assessee has entered into with the borrower of such securities and which is subject to the guidelines issued by the Securities and Exchange Board of India, established under section 3 of the Securities and Exchange Board of India Act, 1992 (15 of 1992), in this regard.

commentary_section.text:
(1)( c)—to enlarge or change the local area of operations—the alteration is to enlarge or change the local area of operation, say beyond the boundaries of a country. In that case, it may invite the ordering of a change in the company’s name, Egyptian Delta Land & Investment Co. Ltd. (1907) 51 Sol. Jo. 211; Indian Mechanical Gold Extracting Co. (1891) Ch. 538.

Combination of some business conveniently or advantageously 11.11-6 Under section 17(1)( d)—to carry on some business which under existing circumstances may conveniently or advantageously be combined with the business of the company—the scope is very wide. Here, the question is of what does and what does not combine with the existing business. "It is essentially a business proposition, whether an additional business can or cannot be conveniently carried on under existing circumstances with the business of the company. The additional business, of course, must not be destructive of or inconsistent with the existing business; it must leave the existing business substantially what it was before; but the additional business may be one which is different from the original business and yet may well be conveniently and advantageously combined with the business which is being carried on." On this basis a tyre company was allowed to enter into the business of banking and finance, Patent Tyre Co., Re. (1923) 2 Ch. 222. A registered and incorporated club to protect cyclists riding on public roads was not allowed to extend their protection business to motorists since the cyclists needed protection mainly against motorists and therefore the alteration sought cannot go with the existing business, Cyclists’ Touring Club, Re (1907) 1 Ch. 269. The choice of the shareholders and the directors about the new business is paramount so long as it does not harm or oppose the existing business, and the company’s financial position is sound. The CLB has evolved the following principles in respect of alteration of the objects clause under section 17(1)(d) :

u A company is normally free to alter its objects, and if the resolution was passed, and particularly where it was unanimously passed, and there came forth no objection from the creditors there is nothing more for CLB to enquire into it;

u It is not necessary that the proposed new business must be ancillary or similar to the existing business, it may be a departure from the existing business, and ‘some business’ used in the clause is some new, or entirely new business;

u The new business proposed to be taken up should not be inconsistent, incongruous or destructive of the existing business, as the word ‘combined’ cannot mean that two mutually destructive business can be combined;

u The company should be in sound financial position without its substratum having not been already eroded to embark on the new business;

u The anticipated advantage to the shareholders requires to be kept in view;

u No hard and fast rule is possible to be prescribed, it has to be decided case-by-case on the facts and circumstances found.

The memorandum of a company can be altered to permit acquisition of the shares in another company, Goneshberi Tea Co. (P.) Ltd., In re [1964] 34 Comp. Cas. 556 (Cal.). A company’s business was mining, and it proposed to enter into selling and erecting various machineries, to carry on hotel business, hire purchase business and real estate business—the alteration was not approved, Bharat Mining Corpn. Ltd., In re [1967] 37 Comp. Cas. 430 (Cal.).

Abandonment of business

11.11-7 Section 17(1)(e ) deals with the alteration on account of restriction or abandonment of the company’s objects. If any company proposes to do so, for whatever reason, it needs a special resolution. In Hampstead Garden Suburb Trust Ltd., In re, [1963] 33 Comp. Cas. 166 (Ch. D), it was proposed to change the nature of the beneficiary and it was held that it was no restriction or abandonment of an object and cannot be confirmed.

Amalgamation and Merger

11.11-8 Section 17(1)(f ) & (g) are about sale or other disposal of the whole or a part of the company’s undertaking and the company’s amalgamation with any other company or body corporate, which it may arise in respect of any company. It is usual for all companies to provide these enabling powers in the incidental objects, and if it is not there present in the memorandum, it needs an alteration in the manner laid down in sections17 to 19 of the Act. If the alteration relates to having the bare power of amalgamation in the proposed alteration, there is no scope for interlocutory relief of injunction, Hari Krishna Lohia v. Hoolungooree Tea Co. Ltd. [1970] 40 Comp. Cas. 458 (Cal.). A condition in the memorandum prohibiting reconstruction or amalgamation is void under section 376.

 combination;

u Likelihood that the combination would result in the removal of a vigorous and effective competitor or competitors in the market;

u Nature and extent of vertical integration in the market;

u Possibility of failing business;

u Nature and extent of innovation;

u Relative advantage, by way of the combination to the economic deve-lopment, by any combination having or likely to have appreciable adverse effect on competition;

u Whether the benefits of the combination outweigh the adverse impact of the combination, if any. Now, in terms of section 6(2), any person or enterprise, who or which proposes to enter into a combination, may, at its option, give notice to the Commission, so far as companies are concerned, within seven days of (a) approval of the proposal relating to merger or amalgamation referred to in C above, by the Board of directors of the enterprises concerned, or, (b) the execution of any agreement or other document for acquisitions covered by A or B above. On receipt of such a notice, the Commission shall deal with it in the manner provided in section 30 : CCI shall examine the notice and if it forms an opinion prima facie as provided in section 29(1), it shall proceed to investigate as per section 29.

Saving from Prohibition

32.6-1 The prohibition against combination laid down in section 6 has no application to share subscription or financing facility or any acquisition, by a public financial institution, foreign institutional investor, bank or venture capital fund, pursuant to any covenant of a loan agreement or investment agreement, provided within seven days from the date of acquisition the details of the acquisition including the details of control, the circumstances for exercise of such control and the consequences of default arising out of such loan agreement or investment agreement, as the case may be, are filed with the Commission, section 6(4) and (5). For purposes of this saving, ‘public financial institution’ refers to a public financial institution specified under section 4A of the Companies Act including a State Financial Corporation and the Industrial Investment Corporation, section 2(p); ‘foreign institutional investor’ has the meaning as assigned to the term in section 115AD of the Income-tax Act, that is, such investor as the Central Government may by notification in the Official Gazette specify; and, ‘venture capital fund’ has the same meaning as assigned to it in section 10(23FB) Explanation (b) of the Income-tax Act, 1961, that is, a fund operating under a trust deed registered under the Registration Act, 1908, or operating as a venture capital scheme made by Unit Trust of India, which has been granted a certificate of registration under the SEBI Act and Regulations and which fulfils the condition as may be specified, with the approval of the Central Government, by SEBI by notification in the Official Gazette.

